        "content": "<p>Hi!</p>\n<p>I've been exploring a classical theory using Lean4 and recently formalized a theorem about a classical geometric equivalence to the Born rule. However, I'm uncertain whether this represents a meaningful classical mechanism, or if it's just a trig identity and nothing more.</p>\n<p>Also, to hopefully keep this question on topic, I'm aware that the first question for any classical theory is how does it hope to solve entanglement. I have formalized a viability theorem that is based on the ER=EPR concept. That would make for a good topic of conversation, but not of <em>this</em> conversation.</p>\n<p>The basic concept is that if we treat particles as <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> objects (a warped sphere, which is incompatible with the standard model), then we can use the Hopf fibration to decompose it into <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> sphere (the shell) with a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(1)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> phase at every point. While we can observe the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> shell because it \"looks\" like a particle, we can only detect if the particle's phase aligns or anti-aligns (spin-up vs spin-down) with a detector.</p>\n<p>When we force two particles to interact, we can calculate the basin of attraction for a detector particle by calculating the surface area that overlaps with the test particle divided by the total surface area of the detector particle. This basin of attraction calculation is exactly the Born rule.</p>\n<p>Here's the code summary (the proofs exist and don't use <code>sorry</code> or <code>axiom</code>, but I stripped them out):</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- MODULE: CGD.Foundations.GaugeGroup</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--------------------------------------------------------------------</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SU2Group</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Complex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">//</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M.conjTranspose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M.det</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- MODULE: CGD.Math.HopfFibration</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--------------------------------------------------------------------</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">The true geometric phase-space volume fraction of the S^2 base manifold.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Calculated formally using Mathlib's interval integration over the sine measure.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hopfPhaseSpaceFraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">theta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">theta</span><span class=\"bp\">..</span><span class=\"n\">Real.pi</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Real.sin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">∫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">..</span><span class=\"n\">Real.pi</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Real.sin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- MODULE: CGD.Quantum.Holonomy.Geometric</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--------------------------------------------------------------------</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">The exact quantum correlation natively emerges from the Cartan-Killing metric</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">of the SU(2) group. For unitary SU(2) elements, 1/2 Tr(A B†) perfectly recovers</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">the cosine of the angle between them.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">litlib_track</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Geometric Bell Correlation\"</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometricBellCorrelation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SU2Group</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℂ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Matrix.trace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A.val</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B.val.conjTranspose</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- MODULE: CGD.Quantum.Measurement.BornRule</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">--------------------------------------------------------------------</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">litlib_track</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"Geometric Born Rule Equivalence\"</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometricBornRuleEquivalence</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">detector</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SU2Group</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometric_val</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">geometricBellCorrelation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">state</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">detector</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">re</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">let</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">theta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Real.arccos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometric_val</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hopfPhaseSpaceFraction</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">theta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometric_val</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometric_val</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Real.cos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">theta</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>So to rephrase my question: is this showing something fundamental about how a measurement must work in a classical theory, or is it just a trig identity?</p>",
